> You can extract that information directly from the `rpart` object; you don’t need to parse the printed text.
> 
> For a fitted tree `fit <- rpart(...)`, the first split is available in `fit$splits[1, ]`, and the node summaries (including the fitted means) are in `fit$frame`.
> 
> Below is a minimal recipe.
> 
> ```r
> library(rpart)
> 
> ## Example: suppose your model is
> ## fit <- rpart(y ~ trade.method.suffix + persist + trade.method, data = dat)
> 
> # 1. Inspect the first split
> fit$splits[1, ]
> #   count ncat       improve       index adj
> #   ...   ...     ...         ...       ...
> 
> var1 <- rownames(fit$splits)[1]   # name of variable used in first split
> var1
> # "trade.method.suffix"
> 
> # 2. Decode which factor levels go to the left/right for the first split
> xlevels <- fit$xlevels[[var1]]    # factor level names in order used by rpart
> xlevels
> 
> sp <- fit$splits[1, ]
> ncat <- sp["ncat"]
> 
> # For factor splits, ncat > 1 and 'index' encodes the split:
> # a positive ncat means: left = levels with bit = 1; right = bit = 0
> idx  <- as.integer(sp["index"])
> 
> # Convert index to binary over the levels
> bits <- as.integer(intToBits(idx))[seq_along(xlevels)]
> 
> left_levels  <- xlevels[bits == 1]
> right_levels <- xlevels[bits == 0]
> 
> left_levels
> right_levels
> ```
