**assistant**:
> Yes. Here’s a small helper that extracts the split information for any internal node of an `rpart` regression tree and returns a tidy list with:
> 
> - `$variable`
> - `$left$condition`
> - `$left$mean_y`
> - `$right$condition`
> - `$right$mean_y`
> 
> It handles:
> 
> - numeric splits like `x < 0.48`
> - factor splits like `trade.method.suffix %in% c("", "-mcpaperall")`
> 
> ## Function
> 
> ```r
> get_rpart_split <- function(fit, node = 1) {
>   if (!inherits(fit, "rpart")) {
>     stop("fit must be an rpart object")
>   }
>   
>   fr <- fit$frame
>   node_names <- rownames(fr)
>   
>   if (!(as.character(node) %in% node_names)) {
>     stop("node not found in fit$frame")
>   }
>   
>   node_row <- match(as.character(node), node_names)
>   
>   if (fr$var[node_row] == "<leaf>") {
>     stop("node is a leaf; no split to extract")
>   }
>   
>   # Internal nodes in frame correspond in order to rows of fit$splits
>   split_row <- cumsum(fr$var != "<leaf>")[node_row]
>   split_info <- fit$splits[split_row, , drop = FALSE]
>   
>   variable <- rownames(split_info)[1]
>   ncat <- split_info[1, "ncat"]
>   index <- split_info[1, "index"]
>   
>   # Children of node n are 2n and 2n+1 in rpart numbering
>   left_node <- as.character(2 * node)
>   right_node <- as.character(2 * node + 1)
>   
>   if (!(left_node %in% node_names) || !(right_node %in% node_names)) {
>     stop("Could not find child nodes in fit$frame")
>   }
>   
>   left_mean_y <- fr[left_node, "yval"]
>   right_mean_y <- fr[right_node, "yval"]
>   
>   if (ncat < 0) {
>     # Numeric split
>     cutpoint <- index
>     left_condition <- paste(variable, "<", format(cutpoint, digits = 12))
>     right_condition <- paste(variable, ">=", format(cutpoint, digits = 12))
>   } else {
>     # Factor split
>     levs <- fit$xlevels[[variable]]
>     if (is.null(levs)) {
>       stop(sprintf("No factor levels found for variable '%s'", variable))
>     }
>     
>     # Decode bitmask in index
>     bits <- as.integer(intToBits(as.integer(index)))[seq_along(levs)]
>     left_levels <- levs[bits == 1]
>     right_levels <- levs[bits == 0]
>     
>     fmt_levels <- function(x) {
>       paste0("c(", paste(sprintf('"%s"', x), collapse = ", "), ")")
>     }
>     
>     left_condition <- paste(variable, "%in%", fmt_levels(left_levels))
>     right_condition <- paste(variable, "%in%", fmt_levels(right_levels))
>   }
>   
>   list(
>     variable = variable,
>     left = list(
>       condition = left_condition,
>       mean_y = unname(left_mean_y)
>     ),
>     right = list(
>       condition = right_condition,
>       mean_y = unname(right_mean_y)
>     )
>   )
> }
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Example usage
> 
> ```r
> res <- get_rpart_split(fit, node = 1)
> str(res)
> ```
> 
> You’d get something like:
> 
> ```r
> $variable
> [1] "trade.method.suffix"
> 
> $left
> $left$condition
> [1] "trade.method.suffix %in% c(\"\")"
> 
> $left$mean_y
> [1] -0.02690459
> 
> 
> $right
> $right$condition
> [1] "trade.method.suffix %in% c(\"-mcpaperall\", \"-mcr2all\")"
> 
> $right$mean_y
> [1] -0.008276888
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Notes
> 
> ### 1. Why `ncat < 0` means numeric
> In `rpart$splits`, numeric splits are encoded with negative `ncat`, while factor splits use positive `ncat`.
> 
> ### 2. Why this works for non-root nodes too
> `fit$frame` stores all nodes; child nodes of node `n` are numbered `2n` and `2n+1`.  
> The only slightly tricky part is matching a node in `fit$frame` to the correct row in `fit$splits`, which is why I use:
> 
> ```r
> split_row <- cumsum(fr$var != "<leaf>")[node_row]
> ```
> 
> That maps the internal node to its split row.
> 
> ### 3. Empty-string factor levels
> If one factor level is `""`, it will print as an empty string in the condition, e.g.:
> 
> ```r
> trade.method.suffix %in% c("")
> ```
> 
> That’s valid and expected.
> 
> ---
